00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.230
So hopefully, there's a
good enough reason for us

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.840
to consider adding yet another.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.570
So before we really start
talking about Vault,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.940
I think it's useful to
talk about the larger

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.130
problem, right-- secret
management as a whole, which

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.750
is really what Vault
attempts to solve.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.584
And so when we talk
about secret management,

00:02:10.584 --> 00:02:13.124
I think the first question, kind
of the most natural question

00:02:13.124 --> 00:02:14.800
is, what exactly
are we managing?

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.750
What is a secret?

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.980
And so one of the things we
talk about is, what is a secret,

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.720
and how is it different
from sensitive information?

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.660
So when we talked about a
piece of secret information,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.470
it's something that allows
us to either authenticate

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.690
or authorize against a service.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.120
So an example might be
a username or password.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.360
We can provide that to, let's
say, a database to authenticate

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.730
a session against that
database, prove our identity,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.104
and then the
database is allowing

00:02:41.104 --> 00:02:43.420
us to request data from it.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.640
Similarly, something
like an API token,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.860
we can present that when
we make a call up to, let's

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.859
say, Google Cloud to read
and write from Blobstore,

00:02:50.859 --> 00:02:52.150
it's authenticating the client.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.000
It's authorizing us to
actually do that request.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.990
And finally, something
like a TLS certificate--

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.870
and you might not
log into a system

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.670
exactly with a TLS certificate.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.260
But you're presenting
it to a client,

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.385
and that's authorizing you
as, you know, let's say,

00:03:04.385 --> 00:03:07.180
www.hashicorp.com,
and so it's allowing

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.300
us to sort of elevate ourselves
into this trusted position.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.330
So any of these types
of materials-- username

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.381
and password, certificates--
are sort of secrets, right?

00:03:16.381 --> 00:03:18.130
And the reason we want
to keep them secret

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.010
is, if they fell into
the wrong party's hands,

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.140
they could use it as a
mechanism to authenticate

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.764
themselves or authorize
themselves to perform actions

00:03:25.764 --> 00:03:26.680
they shouldn't, right?

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.920
They are a mechanism of
access, and so those mechanisms

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.690
need to be tightly kept.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.330
On the other hand, we have
sensitive information, right?

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.730
This is a little different
because it's things

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.630
that you'd like to
keep confidential,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.290
but can't directly be
used to authenticate.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.960
I can't use my customer's
social security number

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.970
to log into the
database, but it doesn't

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.860
mean I'd like that
piece of information

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.950
on the public internet.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.730
So it's important to make this
distinction between sensitive

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.230
things, which Vault
can help us manage--

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.140
and we'll talk a
little bit about how--

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.745
versus secret
things, which Vault

00:03:57.745 --> 00:03:59.830
intends to put inside of it.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:04.330
You use it as a database to
hold onto secret information.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:07.200
So when we go from saying, this
is what a secret is to, then,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.390
talking about secret
management, the way I

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.480
like to talk about it is,
what are the set of questions

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:13.920
we want to be able to answer?

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.900
So when we talk about
secret management,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.579
these are the key ones, right?

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.616
How do our applications
get these secrets?

00:04:19.616 --> 00:04:21.990
My web application boots, it
needs to talk to a database,

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.230
how does it get a
database password?

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.120
On the same hand, how do our
human operators get access?

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.490
If I'm a DBA, and I need
to connect to the database

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.400
to do some maintenance on it
or fix some issue with it,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.207
how do I get that same
database credential?

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:37.290
Then, how do we think about
updating these things?

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.706
We don't want them to be, sort
of, static credentials that

00:04:39.706 --> 00:04:40.500
live forever.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.360
We want it to be
periodically rotating them,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.550
whether for compliance
or just good hygiene.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.049
So how do we think about, if
I change my database password,

00:04:48.049 --> 00:04:49.590
how does that impact
my applications?

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.006
How does that
impact my operators?

00:04:51.006 --> 00:04:53.550
How do I actually
automate that flow?

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.810
Then there are the bad
things that could happen.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.460
What if this thing
is compromised?

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.390
How do I think about
revocation of access?

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.820
How do I do forensics
on who's done what

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.300
when if I'm trying
to figure out,

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.760
are we in a
compromised situation?

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.350
Where has this key leaked?

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.450
And so what do we do in
the event of a compromise,

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.790
everything from that
forensic understanding,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.510
to reacting to it,
to rotating it?

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:18.350
So these are the hard questions
that we'd like answers to.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.480
So the state of the
world, when we generally

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.790
talk about secret
management as a problem

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.890
is not necessarily great.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:28.280
What we often describe
it as is secret sprawl.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.860
This is a scenario in
which secret material

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.320
is sort of sprawled all
over our infrastructure.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.220
It's hard coded in source code.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.580
It's in plain text
in our configuration.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.950
It's checked into
GitHub in plain text.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.680
It just litters everywhere.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.440
And the challenge with this
is it's very decentralized,

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.040
and we have very limited
access controls around it.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.200
Like, who can access
GitHub in our organization?

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.420
Probably anyone with
single sign-on, right?

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:55.370
Who can see this plain
text configuration?

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:55.970
Who knows?

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:58.490
It's not something we're
tightly access-controlling.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.490
And so we have limited
access control around it,

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:02.271
but also even worse visibility.

00:06:02.271 --> 00:06:03.770
So then, if we
actually wanted to do

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.530
an audit of who all has looked
at this particular source

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.040
file in GitHub, do we
actually have an ability

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.310
to do fine-grain auditing
and forensics on that?

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.410
The state of the world--
not necessarily great

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.826
when it comes to answering
some of those secret management

00:06:17.826 --> 00:06:19.302
questions.

00:06:19.302 --> 00:06:21.010
And so this is really
where Vault enters.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.780
Vault's original purpose
was to try and look

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.230
at secret management and say,
can we provide a good answer

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:28.910
to these set of questions?

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:31.940
And so our goals at the
top line are starting

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.430
with a single source of truth.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.560
How do we move away from
this decentralized sprawl

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.352
to a world that is centralized
and managed in a single place?

00:06:40.352 --> 00:06:42.560
And so part of this is really
understanding those two

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.070
different kind of consumers.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.280
There is our
application consumers

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.660
that want programmatic access.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.285
The web server is going to boot,
it wants to fetch a credential

00:06:50.285 --> 00:06:52.550
and do it in an automated way.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.380
We don't want a point
and click interface

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.960
for that-- so API-driven
automated access

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.210
for applications
doing automation.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.910
On the flip side, we still have
our human operators, right?

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.490
They want something
nicer than an API.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:05.630
They want command lines.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:07.760
They want user interfaces.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.537
And so we want these
two access patterns

00:07:09.537 --> 00:07:11.120
to both be really
optimized so that we

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.520
have the single source of
truth, and we want a solution

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:14.870
that's cloud-friendly.

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.630
So what that really means is
it should be easy to operate.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.210
It should be not dependent
on specific hardware

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.651
that we need to deploy
in a private data center.

00:07:22.651 --> 00:07:24.650
It should be [? pure ?]
software that can easily

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:27.419
operate in a cloud environment.

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:28.960
So then, when we
translate that into,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.543
OK, these are Vault's
top-line goals--

00:07:30.543 --> 00:07:33.320
how do we actually achieve
that, the sort of table stakes

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.880
where things start, is just
a secure place to put bits.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.375
We want to put bits in,
have it securely stored,

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:41.500
get those same bits out,
whether that's, let's say,

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.550
database username and password.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.430
And so in-transit, when
clients are talking to Vault,

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:50.140
Vault forces end-to-end
TLS unless you

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:53.320
change it, by default, it asks
for the highest level of 1.2.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.750
And everything at rest
is encrypted and managed

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:56.710
with a AES.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.920
So we have AES 256-bit, both
encrypted and authenticated

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.550
so we can detect
tampering of data at rest.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.500
And this is exposed
to the end user

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.520
as a hierarchical
key value store.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.950
So you can apply
some organization

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.780
around folder structure
and grouping things

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:14.260
together in a way that
makes sense for you.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.780
So to just make this a little
bit less theory, a little more

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.300
concrete, here's an example
from the command line

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:22.090
interface of both writing
and reading a secret.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:24.595
So what we see here, the
first command, is we're

00:08:24.595 --> 00:08:27.230
doing a Vault write
against a path, secret/foo.

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.360
It's like I said, it's a
hierarchical file system.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.950
So in this case, secret is a
directory, and foo is the file,

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:34.850
the leaf node we're writing to.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.350
And then we can
write opaque values.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.140
So we're writing, bar
is equal to bacon.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.260
But you could imagine that
would be username is Armon.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:44.654
Password is 1234, as an example.

00:08:44.654 --> 00:08:46.570
And then we can come
back around, use the CLI,

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.400
and do a read against
that same path.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.350
So we're doing a read
again secret/foo,

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:53.050
and what we see is we're getting
back bar is equal to bacon.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.030
So what this is really
masking for us is really

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:58.330
thinking about, as a client, is
this encrypted over the wire?

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:01.710
The CLI is talking over an
encrypted channel to Vault,

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:02.619
hitting its API.

00:09:02.619 --> 00:09:04.660
And then Vault is
transparently doing the encrypt

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.180
as it writes it out to disk,
doing the decrypt before it

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:08.930
provides it back
to us as a client--

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:10.560
so doing the right
things, making sure

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.310
we're doing this with
hygiene, but kind of

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:15.420
making this easy as an end user.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.790
Just to illustrate the
equivalence of that,

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.690
here's the same example as
what we just did on the CLI,

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.580
but hitting Vault's API instead.

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.930
So like I said, one
primary consumer Vault

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:28.915
is our applications
or automation tools.

00:09:28.915 --> 00:09:31.290
And they want to consume it
programmatically with an API.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.706
So here's just a simple example
of doing cURL, where we're

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:38.430
basically doing a write against
the path same path of foo

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.380
and encoding the data
that we want stored,

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.720
and then doing a readback
against the same thing.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.910
So it's meant to be a
simple RESTful API that's

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:47.950
easy to integrate with.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.840
And so that's one level.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.234
That's table stakes is
putting our secrets somewhere

00:09:53.234 --> 00:09:54.400
that we know it's encrypted.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.770
We have audit logging and
access control around it--

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.340
all of that kind of good stuff.

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.060
But there's still a bunch
of hard questions, right?

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:04.000
There are questions like,
what if an operator connects

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.130
to Vault, and they just
find it annoying to have

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:06.940
to go to a Vault.
So they're going

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.730
to copy everything out and put
it in passwords.txt so they

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.230
don't have to bother to log in?

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:12.961
Or an application starts--

00:10:12.961 --> 00:10:15.460
and I'm sure we've never seen
this-- the first thing it does

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:17.290
is write all of
its configuration

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.120
out to standard out
or its log file.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.150
So it just reads all of the
database username and password,

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:23.691
dumps that to disk, and ships
that off to our central logging

00:10:23.691 --> 00:10:25.120
system in plain text.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:26.890
And the third sort
of a classic case

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.480
is we had a diagnostic
error, right?

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.560
It's a 500 error on rendering,
or we're in a debug mode.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.910
And it says, here's my
database username and password,

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.310
by the way, if this
helps you with debugging.

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.452
So the thing you
find is the entities

00:10:39.452 --> 00:10:41.410
we're giving these
credentials to, whether it's

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:44.890
applications or operators, do
a terrible job keeping secrets.

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:48.070
So this kind of raises an
interesting challenge of,

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:50.000
how do we still
protect these secrets,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.410
even though all of these
scenarios are very likely?

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.080
So Vault's approach
to this, the way

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.120
Vault thinks about
this problem is

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:01.180
the challenge is we have these
long-lived static credentials.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.430
And the challenge with a
long-lived static credentials

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:06.880
is if they leak anywhere, ever,
either through passwords.txt,

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:09.600
or through a log file, or
through a diagnostic page,

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:10.960
it's game over.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.474
We've arranged it
as a zero-sum game.

00:11:13.474 --> 00:11:15.640
It's either a perfectly
kept secret, or the secret's

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.170
out there, and we're hosed.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:20.500
So how do we reduce the stakes?

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:23.750
How do we make this less
severe than all or nothing?

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.200
And the way you have to do it
is making secrets ephemeral.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.533
You don't want to have these
long-lived credentials that

00:11:28.533 --> 00:11:29.351
are valid forever.

00:11:29.351 --> 00:11:31.600
You want these things to be
short-lived, time-bounded,

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.670
things so that, even if
they get logged to disk--

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:36.400
oops-- they're only
valid for so long

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.082
before we've moved on to a
different set of credentials.

00:11:39.082 --> 00:11:40.540
So Vault's approach
to this is what

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:42.140
we call dynamic secrets, right?

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.680
And the idea is, as opposed
to static secrets, what

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:47.560
we want to do is generate
a unique set of credentials

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:48.580
on demand.

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.160
So when a web server says, I
want a database credential,

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.220
we generate that credential as
in point-in-time right there.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.890
We have a unique audit trail
now that ties us back and says,

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.260
this web server has
this unique credential.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.600
And by the way, that's
valid only for 24 hours.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:05.140
So when the web server renders
a 500 diagnostic page and leaks

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:07.240
out our database password,
we've time-bounded

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.530
how long that credential's
valid before moving on

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:10.679
to another credential.

00:12:10.679 --> 00:12:12.220
And if there is a
breach, and we know

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:15.905
that that credential's exposed,
we can revoke it early.

00:12:15.905 --> 00:12:18.280
So what does this actually
look like from the perspective

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.060
of a client?

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:20.860
They're just doing
a read against Vault

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:22.943
in the same way we just
saw before where they just

00:12:22.943 --> 00:12:24.164
did a read of secret/foo.

00:12:24.164 --> 00:12:26.080
And behind the scenes,
Vault is then going out

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:27.502
and talking to the endpoint.

00:12:27.502 --> 00:12:29.710
So it's going and talking
to the database and saying,

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:31.430
create a new dynamic credential.

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:33.430
It's creating an audit
trail of that credential,

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.359
and then it's providing that
credential back to the user.

00:12:36.359 --> 00:12:37.900
In this example,
I'm using a database

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:40.390
because I think it's
sort of an intuitive one.

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:42.610
But you can imagine
this exact same diagram

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.860
where you replace the
database with any sort

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:47.500
of endpoint system that
supports credentialing, right?

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.427
A prime example would
be Google Cloud, itself.

00:12:50.427 --> 00:12:52.510
So I want to give my
application a credential that

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:55.116
lets it read and write from
Blobstore, as an example.

00:12:55.116 --> 00:12:57.490
But I don't necessarily want
to give it a credential that

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.840
lives forever.

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.360
I want to give it a credential
that's valid for 24 hours,

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:03.359
and I'm rotating that
credential all the time.

00:13:03.359 --> 00:13:05.900
So in that same setting, Vault
would connect to Google Cloud,

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:07.420
generate a short-lived
credential,

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:09.795
create the audit trail, and
provide it to the application

00:13:09.795 --> 00:13:12.190
or end user, maybe a developer's
doing some debugging,

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:13.660
so this example holds.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.911
So when we talk about this
secret back end approach--

00:13:19.911 --> 00:13:21.160
so far we talked about Google.

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.820
We talked about using it
with a database-type system.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.710
But it's really a general idea.

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:28.690
The general idea
is, any system that

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:31.939
uses credentialing to manage
authentication or authorization

00:13:31.939 --> 00:13:33.730
has this similar sort
of a challenge, which

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:36.310
is our consumers need that
credential so they can consume

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:37.567
the end point system.

00:13:37.567 --> 00:13:39.400
But we don't want these
things to be static.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.290
And so our goal
with Vault is, this

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:42.550
is actually a plug-in point.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:45.970
It's an extensibility point
where, over time, we're

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:48.940
constantly adding new
systems and adding support

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:52.490
for them to be consumed
in this dynamic way.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:54.460
So cloud providers
are one great example

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.459
where they need API
tokens to interface with,

00:13:56.459 --> 00:13:58.000
and maybe are giving
those to humans,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.320
maybe are giving
those to applications,

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:01.520
maybe your automation
systems are consuming it.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.103
How do we make sure
that we don't have

00:14:03.103 --> 00:14:05.064
a forever token in Jenkins?

00:14:05.064 --> 00:14:06.730
Databases are great
a example of systems

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:07.977
of a record that have data.

00:14:07.977 --> 00:14:10.060
But then you get into all
sorts of other systems--

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:13.990
messaging queues, things
like RabbitMQ, SSH.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.330
I think we could probably--
maybe I'll spare everyone

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:17.800
from raising hands.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:20.530
But I'm sure many of us
have seen the master shared

00:14:20.530 --> 00:14:22.900
PEM file with a few
hundred developers

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:26.140
having access to the same PEM
that secures a huge fleet.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:28.270
And so that's an
interesting challenge

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:30.940
where we have this shared
mechanism, this PEM file,

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.820
that too many people
have access to.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:37.402
So the list goes on and on.

00:14:37.402 --> 00:14:38.860
Other stuff that
we don't have time

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:40.360
to touch onto in
much more detail

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.450
is classic security
bread and butter--

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.590
the three A's.

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:47.290
As you'd expect, Vault has
many different mechanisms

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:48.686
to authenticate against it.

00:14:48.686 --> 00:14:50.560
So whether it's an app
running in Kubernetes,

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.150
and we're tying
into the platform,

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:53.830
it's a VM just running
in Google Cloud,

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:56.970
or it's a user who's using
LDAP to authenticate--

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:58.660
those many different
authentication

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.940
mechanisms-- all
of that comes into,

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.350
then, a central
authorization system.

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:04.870
So there's a
central way in Vault

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.000
where you authorize who
is allowed to do what.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.070
So one huge goal with
Vault is, how do we

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:10.726
get to least privilege?

00:15:10.726 --> 00:15:12.100
You should only
be able to access

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:14.590
exactly what you need,
not everything because you

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:15.690
were able to log in.

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:18.292
So Vault has a very
fine grain AuthZ system

00:15:18.292 --> 00:15:19.750
that lets you scope
down who can do

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:22.090
what based on their identity.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:23.830
And then lastly
is rich auditing.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:28.554
So we want to have that audit
trail of who did what when.

00:15:28.554 --> 00:15:29.540
Cool.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.389
And with that, I'm going
to hand it back off

00:15:31.389 --> 00:15:33.680
to Seth who's going to talk
to us about how we actually

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.230
use this and operationalize
this within Kubernetes.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:37.514
Thank you.

00:15:37.514 --> 00:15:39.899
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:43.130
SETH VARGO: Great.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:44.000
Thank you, Armon.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.710
So the goal of the
next section here

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:47.870
is to really answer
two questions.

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:49.550
Why would I run
this on Kubernetes?

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:50.360
Is it a good idea?

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:51.080
Should I?

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:52.405
And how do I do it?

00:15:52.405 --> 00:15:53.780
The first answer
is yes, so we'll

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:56.870
move onto the second question.

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:59.340
So what does this
actually look like?

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:02.690
So the following diagram
here is also captured as code

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.862
on GitHub,
github.com/sethvargo/vaultongke.

00:16:06.862 --> 00:16:08.820
But I want to walk through
it quickly together.

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:10.340
And before I do,
I want to mention

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:14.300
that this follows the
HashiCorp best practices

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.349
guide for production deployments
and production hardening.

00:16:17.349 --> 00:16:19.640
This was built in collaboration
with some of the Google

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:21.770
Security engineers as
well as some of the people

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:24.470
on the SIC security
team on Kubernetes.

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:28.460
So it represents what we like to
think of as the best-practices

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:32.210
way to run Vault on GKE
or any Kubernetes cluster.

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:34.575
So at the top level, we have
some type of load balancer.

00:16:34.575 --> 00:16:36.200
In this example,
we're using cloud load

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:38.900
balancing because we're making
this Vault server publicly

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:39.944
accessible.

00:16:39.944 --> 00:16:41.360
In your own
environment, you might

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:44.030
want to use VPCs or
VPN-type networking,

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:47.430
where it's only accessible
in certain subnets.

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:49.850
And then we have a highly
available Vault cluster.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:53.150
So we might have three, five,
seven servers all running

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:55.624
behind this load balancer.

00:16:55.624 --> 00:16:57.290
And then below that,
we have, obviously,

00:16:57.290 --> 00:16:58.550
the Kubernetes engine itself.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.910
And then we're using Google
Cloud Storage as the underlying

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:02.630
storage back end.

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:04.849
We also support
Google Cloud Spanner.

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:06.530
And we are using
Google Cloud KMS

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:09.200
to do the initial encryption
of the initial root token

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:10.621
and the initial unsealed keys.

00:17:10.621 --> 00:17:12.079
So let's walk
through some of those

00:17:12.079 --> 00:17:14.190
in a little bit more detail.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:17.979
So first, we're using a
dedicated Kubernetes cluster.

00:17:17.979 --> 00:17:19.520
And there are a few
reasons for this.

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:23.540
But we don't want Vault running
alongside the same Kubernetes

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:25.824
cluster where your
applications are running.

00:17:25.824 --> 00:17:28.490
In fact, we might even recommend
running it in a separate Google

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:30.950
project that only the security
teams and the networking

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:31.610
teams--

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:33.260
least privilege access.

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:36.650
And to the other clusters and
other application developers,

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:39.867
Vault is just a thing
at an IP address.

00:17:39.867 --> 00:17:41.450
We're leveraging
Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:43.366
Again, we could leverage
Google Cloud Spanner.

00:17:43.366 --> 00:17:45.952
But both of those storage back
ends support high availability.

00:17:45.952 --> 00:17:47.410
As a production
deployment, we need

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:49.010
this to be highly available.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:50.480
There's no reason
why we wouldn't.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.490
So we're going to leverage
Google Cloud Storage

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:55.250
for our high availability
and our storage back end.

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:58.020
Another advantage there is
that we can back that data up.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.150
We can do replication.

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:02.930
We can do customer-provided
encryption keys as well.

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:05.180
We're going to leverage
Google Cloud KMS.

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:07.130
Everyone taking
a picture-- nice.

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:08.797
We're going to leverage
Google Cloud KMS

00:18:08.797 --> 00:18:11.088
because our Vault servers
are going to be automatically

00:18:11.088 --> 00:18:12.290
initialized and unsealed.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:15.140
So those initial unsealed keys
and that initial route token

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.360
eventually need to get into
the hands of an operator

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:19.070
or an automation
system, but we don't

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:20.550
want them stored in plain text.

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.341
So we're going to
leverage Google Cloud KMS

00:18:22.341 --> 00:18:26.870
to encrypt those values and put
them in the storage back end.

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:29.360
Additionally, we're leveraging
our own custom certificate

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:30.920
authority for TLS.

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:32.780
So one of the questions
I get is, like, oh,

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:33.890
why wouldn't you
use Let's Encrypt?

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:35.780
Let's Encrypt is
amazing, but the problem

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.030
is, I don't actually want
to trust any certificate

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:38.930
on the internet.

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.004
Instead, I want to use
mutual TLS validation,

00:18:41.004 --> 00:18:42.920
and I want to use my own
certificate authority

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:45.620
and only trust certificates
signed by that authority, not

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:47.259
even my system certificates.

00:18:47.259 --> 00:18:48.800
This helps secure
Vault and make sure

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:50.450
that only people
with the certificates

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:52.040
and the proper
certificate authority

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:56.030
have the ability to
communicate with the server.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:58.322
Additionally, we're
leveraging L4 load balancing.

00:18:58.322 --> 00:19:00.280
Instead of using something
like the Global Load

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:02.950
Balancer, the GLB, we're
actually using a regional load

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:03.730
balancers.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.550
And the key reason
here is that an L7 load

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:09.057
balancer is going to do
the TLS termination for us.

00:19:09.057 --> 00:19:10.390
And we don't actually want that.

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:13.450
We want Vault to do the
TLS termination, primarily,

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:16.330
so that Vault can handle
mutual TLS authentication.

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:18.395
But also, we don't want
to decrypt data and then

00:19:18.395 --> 00:19:20.770
have a different re-encrypted
connection on the back end,

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:21.100
right?

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:23.560
Using an L4 balancer lets us
pass that through directly

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:27.220
to Vault and lets Vault
handle its own TLS.

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:29.320
And if Vault ever rotates
those TLS certificates

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.270
that it's using, those
automatically happen.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:32.670
We don't have to
also rotate them

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:35.560
in an upstream load balancer.

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:38.260
And then lastly-- and this
is a really key point--

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:41.220
we want to think of Vault
as a service, right?

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:44.140
So in my internal operations
team, or my internal security

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:46.057
team, yes, I'm running
Vault under Kubernetes,

00:19:46.057 --> 00:19:47.931
and I'm leveraging a
lot of the functionality

00:19:47.931 --> 00:19:49.330
that Kubernetes provides.

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:52.330
But ultimately, I'm delivering
secrets as a service or secrets

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:53.770
management as a service.

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:57.250
So there is Vault and an
API and an IP address.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:59.110
And users ultimately
shouldn't care

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:02.860
whether Vault is running on a
VM or a container or bare metal.

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:04.160
It doesn't really matter.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:06.880
So we really want to think about
this as Kubernetes is providing

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:08.710
the building blocks
that let us run Vault

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.890
as a highly-available service.

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:13.370
And we get some
really cool benefits.

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:16.390
One of them is you might
see that we're going

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.050
to leverage a StatefulSet here.

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:19.600
And the first thing you
might think of is, wait,

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:20.516
this isn't a database.

00:20:20.516 --> 00:20:21.730
There's actually no state.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:23.200
Vault's state is
stored elsewhere.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:24.100
It's stored in GCS.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.524
Why would he use a StatefulSet?

00:20:25.524 --> 00:20:27.190
And there are a few
reasons why we might

00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:28.630
want to use a StatefulSet.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:30.390
First, it gives us
predictable naming.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:33.760
Our actual container names
will be like vault-0, vault-1,

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:34.420
vault-2.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:36.130
This is really
helpful for debugging,

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.020
audit logging, et cetera.

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:41.740
The real reason, though, is
that the StatefulSets give us

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:44.360
exactly-once semantics for free.

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:47.204
So as our servers are starting
up, one will start up,

00:20:47.204 --> 00:20:49.120
it will unseal itself,
it will be ready to go.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:50.744
As soon as its health
check is passing,

00:20:50.744 --> 00:20:52.210
the next one will move along.

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:54.130
So we actually avoid
any real-life [? race ?]

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:56.770
conditions with
initialization or unsealing.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:58.960
The same is true for upgrading.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:01.660
You don't want to upgrade all
of your Vault clusters at once.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.427
You generally want to upgrade
one, and let the leadership

00:21:04.427 --> 00:21:06.760
transition to a new server,
upgrade that one, et cetera,

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:08.560
until the entire
cluster is upgraded.

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.750
This is true with any software.

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:13.870
Using StatefulSets, a single
change to the YAML file,

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:17.470
or whatever you're using for the
description of this deployment,

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:19.550
automatically does
a rolling update.

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:21.130
And if one of them
fails, it'll stop.

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:22.570
We get a free circuit
breaker pattern.

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:24.236
So there's a lot of
reasons why we might

00:21:24.236 --> 00:21:26.330
want to use a StatefulSet here.

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:29.230
Additionally, we're leveraging
anti-affinity rules.

00:21:29.230 --> 00:21:32.020
So anti-affinity rules
are a Kubernetes future

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:34.690
which let us do things
that affect or influence

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:36.550
the pod scheduling.

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:39.452
By default, if we did
not have this YAML here,

00:21:39.452 --> 00:21:41.410
Kubernetes would put all
three Vault containers

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:42.970
on the first node.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.190
And that's not highly available.

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:49.310
If that one VM dies, we lose
all of our Vault servers.

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:51.550
So what we're doing is
leveraging anti-affinity,

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:54.290
basically, suggesting to
Kubernetes, or in this example

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:56.800
requiring Kubernetes,
to spread these out

00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:59.440
across all of our
available nodes or hosts.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:02.440
This means, if one VM
dies or two VMs die,

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:03.940
that our Vault servers--

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:05.380
the actual containers
themselves--

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:07.270
are spread across the node pool.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:11.710
So this adds an additional
layer of availability for us.

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:13.890
We're also leveraging
auto unsealing.

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:16.350
The auto unsealing is
also an auto initializer.

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:18.840
This allows you to spin up
a Vault cluster very quickly

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:20.270
without operator intervention.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:21.900
And the keys will be
encrypted with KMS

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:25.402
and put in the storage back end.

00:22:25.402 --> 00:22:26.860
We're also doing
some little things

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:27.880
that make a big difference.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:29.505
For example, we're
giving the container

00:22:29.505 --> 00:22:31.000
the IPC_LOCK capability.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.069
By default, Vault
supports memory locking.

00:22:33.069 --> 00:22:35.110
This is something the
Vault does in its codebase.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:36.776
Containers don't give
you that privilege

00:22:36.776 --> 00:22:39.010
by default, so we
explicitly ask Kubernetes

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:41.650
to give our containers the
ability to do memory locking--

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:45.410
again, just an added
layer of security.

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:47.900
And then our readiness
check is basically

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:51.170
asking Vault for its own
health, but we explicitly

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.600
want to make sure that
our standbys are also

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:54.775
added to the load balancer.

00:22:54.775 --> 00:22:56.150
Because this
readiness check here

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:57.942
is going to determine
when the container is

00:22:57.942 --> 00:22:59.941
able to receive requests
from the load balancer,

00:22:59.941 --> 00:23:02.420
and we want to make sure that
our standby servers are also

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:05.150
able to receive requests so
that they can forward them

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:07.630
appropriately to the leader.

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:09.710
Notice that we're
communicating via TLS.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:14.430
And then lastly, as
we saw in the diagram,

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:15.630
we have this load balancer.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:18.540
Again, this might be an internal
load balancer in your own VPC.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.950
Or it might be a
public-facing load balancer.

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:23.800
I don't recommend that, but
there's no reason it can't be.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:25.940
So with that, let's
jump over to demo one--

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:28.920
so if we can switch
over to the laptop.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:29.930
All right.

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:30.680
Here we go.

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:32.750
Did everyone pray
to the demo gods?

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:33.481
Here we go.

00:23:33.481 --> 00:23:33.980
All right.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:36.230
So the first demo
here, I'm just going

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:38.830
to show you what it's like
to run Vault as a service

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:41.780
on Kubernetes, basically, using
those exact YAML files that I

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:42.490
just showed you.

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:44.990
First, I want to show you, I
have a Kubernetes cluster here.

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:48.020
It's running on GKE.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:57.710
So I'll go to Kubernetes
Engine, and we'll take a look

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.260
at the workloads here.

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:02.537
And there's nothing, right?

00:24:02.537 --> 00:24:03.620
We have no workloads here.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:05.427
We haven't deployed Vault yet.

00:24:05.427 --> 00:24:07.010
So what I'm going
to do is, over here,

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:12.656
I'll just take a look
at my vault.yaml.

00:24:12.656 --> 00:24:15.030
And you can see that this is
very, very similar to what I

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:16.450
just showed you on the slides.

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:18.408
There are some additional
pieces of information

00:24:18.408 --> 00:24:21.122
here, like, I'm deploying
a replica set of three.

00:24:21.122 --> 00:24:22.830
So I'm going to have
three Vault servers.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:24.204
Again, there's
that anti-affinity

00:24:24.204 --> 00:24:25.639
that we saw before.

00:24:25.639 --> 00:24:27.430
And all of this is open
source, by the way.

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:30.114
So don't feel like you have to
capture all of this right now.

00:24:30.114 --> 00:24:31.530
We have our first
container, which

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.270
is our init container
that's going

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:35.410
to do the Vault initialization.

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:36.960
And then we have
some configuration

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:38.490
for that container.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:40.720
And then we have the
actual Vault server itself.

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:42.720
We're pulling that directly
from the Docker hub.

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:44.677
Again, there's that
IPC_LOCK capability.

00:24:44.677 --> 00:24:46.260
And we're opening
some container ports

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:48.570
that will be available
for requests--

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:50.460
some basic memory
and CPU requests,

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:52.071
and then, again,
some configuration.

00:24:52.071 --> 00:24:53.820
And here, you can see
where we're actually

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:56.160
supplying Vault its
startup configuration

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:58.532
via the magic VAULT_LOCAL_CONFIG
environment variable.

00:24:58.532 --> 00:25:00.990
So we don't have to worry about
writing out a file to disk.

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:04.320
We do this all in the
environment variable.

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:08.261
So with that, let me go
ahead and apply this.

00:25:08.261 --> 00:25:10.260
I'm terrible at typing,
so I wrote fancy scripts

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:11.352
for you all.

00:25:11.352 --> 00:25:12.810
So what this is
going to do is it's

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.684
going to just grab that
Kubernetes end point,

00:25:14.684 --> 00:25:17.670
and it's going to apply
kubeclt apply-f that particular

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:18.880
StatefulSet.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:21.000
So what we should see
here in a second--

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.530
and this is where everyone's
prayers would be helpful--

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:24.576
OK, there we go.

00:25:24.576 --> 00:25:26.200
So these pods are
actually starting up.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:28.390
And you'll notice that 1 of
3 of them are starting up.

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:30.598
And that's because we're
using the StatefulSet, which

00:25:30.598 --> 00:25:33.320
is doing that one at a time,
exactly-once semantics.

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:34.650
So vault-0 is running.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.091
It has some
warnings, but I think

00:25:36.091 --> 00:25:37.590
that's just because
it's booting up.

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:43.220
And if we go back, we'll
start to see, hopefully--

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.020
OK, vault-1 is starting as well.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:48.490
And then once we get to
vault-3, this entire replica set

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:49.640
will be happy.

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:51.640
And at that point, we
will have a vault cluster.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:53.590
Because what's actually
happening, under the hood,

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:55.570
is we're not just running
that Vault container,

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:57.700
we're also running that
Vault init container.

00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:00.160
And because this is a
fresh, new container,

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:03.340
it's actually connecting
to the storage back end,

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:04.960
doing the
initialization, putting

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:08.290
those unsealed keys encrypted
via KMS into that storage back

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:10.259
end, as well as the
initial route token.

00:26:10.259 --> 00:26:11.800
And then the other
servers, when they

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:13.420
start up, they look,
and they say, oh,

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:14.830
I'm already initialized.

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:17.350
They grab those encryption
keys and do automatic unsealing

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:18.100
for themselves.

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:19.600
If we were to
scale-up or scale-down

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:21.880
the cluster, because the
initialization has already

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.710
happened, the initializer
will just unseal them.

00:26:24.710 --> 00:26:28.087
It won't actually do the
initialization anymore.

00:26:28.087 --> 00:26:30.670
Let's go ahead and refresh this
and check where our status is.

00:26:30.670 --> 00:26:32.652
I see a green check
mark, which is great.

00:26:32.652 --> 00:26:35.110
I'm going to go ahead and jump
over to Google Cloud Storage

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:38.780
here, just real quick
to show you all.

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:41.390
So this is the storage
bucket that I'm using.

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.760
And in here, you'll
see the folders here--

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:46.200
core and sys, which
are created by Vault.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:48.650
But then the root token
and the unsealed keys

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.720
are both encrypted
via Google Cloud KMS.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:53.340
And then they're stored in this
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.590
So when I want to interact
with Vault, as an operator,

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:57.550
I need to download
those via like gsutil

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:00.230
or via the Google
Cloud API, decrypt

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:03.170
them using Google Cloud KMS
and the proper IAM permissions,

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:05.089
and then I can interact
with Vault directly.

00:27:05.089 --> 00:27:07.130
So let me go ahead and
switch back to Kubernetes,

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:10.290
and I'll show you what
all of that looks like.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:11.621
So I'm going to jump over here.

00:27:11.621 --> 00:27:13.120
I have another
magical shell script,

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:17.044
which I will show everyone.

00:27:17.044 --> 00:27:18.460
So this is my
magical shell script

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:20.160
where I super can't type.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:23.710
But basically, all of this does
is it grabs the CA certificate.

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:25.630
Again, we're using a custom CA--

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:28.550
so putting the output of
that, the address to my Vault

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:29.050
cluster.

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:30.760
And I provisioned this Vault
cluster with Terraform,

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:32.482
so I'm delegating
to Terraform there--

00:27:32.482 --> 00:27:33.940
and then the token
decrypt command,

00:27:33.940 --> 00:27:35.920
which is just a really
long gsutl command

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:38.649
with some basic Z4
and some G Cloud KMS.

00:27:38.649 --> 00:27:40.690
So what I'm going to go
ahead and do is run this.

00:27:40.690 --> 00:27:42.770
And again, this is
all open source.

00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:43.360
Oh, look.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:45.099
See, I left myself
this note because I

00:27:45.099 --> 00:27:46.390
knew I was going to mess it up.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:51.580
This is when you know
you've done too many demos.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.205
So this takes a little
bit because it's

00:27:53.205 --> 00:27:56.070
actually making about 5 or
10 API calls, but great.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.620
Now, what I can do is
I can actually just run

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:03.411
env grep vault. We
see that we have

00:28:03.411 --> 00:28:05.660
our VAULT_TOKEN, our VAULT_ADDR,
and our VAULT_CACERT.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:07.910
These are magic environment
variables the Vault reads.

00:28:07.910 --> 00:28:10.970
I can also supply them
via the CLI, but I'm lazy.

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:12.920
So now what I can
do is, hopefully, I

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:14.142
can run Vault status.

00:28:14.142 --> 00:28:14.850
And look at that.

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:17.000
We now have a Vault
cluster up and running--

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:19.100
five key shares, a
threshold of three.

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:22.160
It's in high-availability mode,
and this particular node I hit

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:22.910
is in standby.

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:25.040
So if we look at that
second to last line there,

00:28:25.040 --> 00:28:28.720
we'll see that this particular
node that we hit is in standby.

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:31.640
If I run Vault status again,
now I hit the active node,

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:32.300
coincidentally.

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:34.880
Because we're doing round-robin
load balancing on that load

00:28:34.880 --> 00:28:36.887
balancer there.

00:28:36.887 --> 00:28:38.470
Anytime I make a
request to a standby,

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:40.360
that standby will automatically
forward the request

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:41.230
to the active leader.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:43.063
So I don't have to know
where the leader is.

00:28:43.063 --> 00:28:45.680
I just know the load
balancer address.

00:28:45.680 --> 00:28:48.620
So this is how you run Vault
as a service on Kubernetes.

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:51.390
If I kill one of these pods,
it will automatically restart.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:53.000
It'll maintain a
quorum of three.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.820
When it comes back online, it
will be automatically unsealed.

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:58.640
And until that point, it won't
be added to the load balancer

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:01.320
as well, which is why that
health check is so important.

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:03.194
So with that, can we
