00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:17.490
So now we have to
forget everything.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:19.590
Forget everything you
just talked about.

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:23.430
Because the next section is,
now that I have Vault running,

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:25.110
how do I connect to
it from Kubernetes?

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:28.770
But I want you to forget that
Vault is running in Kubernetes.

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:31.200
Think, it's running in
some third-party service

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:34.440
like Heroku, or it's
running on VM somewhere.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:37.290
Forget that Vault is
running in Kubernetes.

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:40.470
Instead, Vault is just a thing
available at an IP address.

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:42.120
Everything we're
about to do can work

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:43.590
with any Kubernetes cluster.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:45.090
We're going to use
GKE, but it could

00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:47.820
work with your on-premise
Kubernetes cluster, et cetera.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:51.510
So again, forget that Vault is
running on GKE or Kubernetes.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:53.604
It's just a thing
at an IP address.

00:29:53.604 --> 00:29:55.020
Because the next
thing we're going

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:58.790
to do is create another
Kubernetes cluster.

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.600
All right.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:01.290
Clicky.

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:01.790
All right.

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:04.661
So very quickly, we have to
talk about Vault authentication

00:30:04.661 --> 00:30:05.160
methods.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:08.130
So Armon mentioned this very
briefly in the beginning.

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:10.230
Authentication in
Vault is the process

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:12.960
by which users or
machines supply

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:16.620
information to Vault. Vault
verifies that information with,

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:20.040
either internal data or
some external third party,

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:21.810
and then, assuming
that it is valid,

00:30:21.810 --> 00:30:26.010
converts that into a token with
policy authorization attached

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:27.830
to it.

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:31.190
Here's an example of what that
looks like in a graphic form.

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:33.250
We have the Vault server
in the middle, which

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:36.040
would receive a
request from a user

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:37.702
or a machine or an application.

00:30:37.702 --> 00:30:39.160
It would then take
that information

00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:41.710
to this third-party system.

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:44.200
Assuming that's successful,
it would go to its own policy

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:46.164
engine and map that
to a Vault policy,

00:30:46.164 --> 00:30:48.580
and then return the token,
which is kind of like a session

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:51.550
ID or a session token to the
user, which would then be

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:53.600
used for all future requests.

00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:55.240
It's like logging
into a website.

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:57.290
You know, sometimes you log in
with a username and a password,

00:30:57.290 --> 00:30:58.874
and your browser
sets a session ID.

00:30:58.874 --> 00:31:00.790
But sometimes you can
also log in with Twitter

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:02.430
or log in with GitHub.

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:06.700
Vault is conceptually
similar to that.

00:31:06.700 --> 00:31:08.560
So what does that look
like with Kubernetes?

00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:10.268
Well, it's actually
very straightforward.

00:31:10.268 --> 00:31:12.880
We just replace that third-party
system with a token reviewer

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:15.130
API from Kubernetes.

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:17.320
So a user, or in
this case a service

00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:19.870
account representing
a user or a pod,

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:22.600
makes a request to the HashiCorp
Vault server with their service

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:24.590
account token, their JWT token.

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:27.750
That JWT token is given
to the TokenReviewer API.

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:30.730
The TokenReviewer API verifies
that JWT token's validity,

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:32.045
and time stamp, et cetera.

00:31:32.045 --> 00:31:34.670
And assuming that's successful,
it goes back to Vault and says,

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:37.289
yeah, this looks great.

00:31:37.289 --> 00:31:39.330
Vault then goes to its
own internal policy engine

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:40.200
and says, oh, OK.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:42.930
Well, the things in this
particular namespace

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:45.240
get this particular
policy attached to them.

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:47.950
Here's a Vault token with
that policy attached.

00:31:47.950 --> 00:31:50.400
And this is how the
Kubernetes auth method works

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:51.916
at a very, very high level.

00:31:51.916 --> 00:31:53.290
There are technical
details here,

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:54.790
which we're not
going to get into.

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:58.300
But this is, at a very high
level, how this functions.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:02.260
So to do this, you could write
your own little BASH script.

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:04.660
Or I've actually open-sourced
the Vault Kubernetes

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:07.960
authenticator, which does
this whole process for you.

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:09.700
It will grab your
service account

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:12.300
from var/run,
Kubernetes, secrets,

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:13.875
the service account JWT token.

00:32:13.875 --> 00:32:15.250
It will post that
up to the Vault

00:32:15.250 --> 00:32:17.920
server which is configured
via an environment variable.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:19.510
It will get the
token back, and it'll

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:22.030
put it in an in-memory
volume mount for you.

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:24.280
So then, all your applications
and services have to do

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:27.142
is extract that token from
that in-memory volume mount.

00:32:27.142 --> 00:32:28.600
So you don't have
to actually think

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.550
about Vault authentication
in Kubernetes anymore.

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:31.840
You could just
leverage this and know

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:34.150
that the token will be there,
assuming authentication

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:35.500
was successful.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:38.530
It runs as an init container,
not a sidecar container.

00:32:38.530 --> 00:32:43.350
So it must succeed in order for
the other containers to run.

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:46.242
Then we leverage tools
like Consul Template.

00:32:46.242 --> 00:32:47.700
So despite its
name, you don't need

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:49.470
Consul to use Consul Template.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:51.270
But Consul Template
is a tool that

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:54.210
is designed to provide a
configuration language by which

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:57.510
we extract secrets and
credentials from Vault

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:01.590
and present them on-disk or on
a temp disk or an in-memory disk

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:05.220
to an application or service
without the application

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.710
or service knowing that it's
communicating with Vault.

00:33:07.710 --> 00:33:10.290
Many of you have brownfield
applications or, even,

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:11.954
greenfield applications
that you likely

00:33:11.954 --> 00:33:14.370
don't want to directly integrate
with Vault. You might not

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:16.980
want to call the Vault API
directly using the Golang

00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:18.524
library or the Ruby library.

00:33:18.524 --> 00:33:20.190
Instead, you might
want to just continue

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:22.290
to leverage a file
system or environment

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:24.010
variables for credentials.

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:26.040
And we like that abstraction
because then you're

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:27.840
not tightly coupling
your applications

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:29.070
to a secret manager.

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:31.080
Instead, you're saying,
my config is on disk,

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:33.350
or my config is from an
environment variable.

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:35.250
What Consul Template
allows us to do

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:37.200
is to provide that
same experience,

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:39.907
but under the hood, as
a sidecar application,

00:33:39.907 --> 00:33:41.490
it is actually
managing the connection

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:43.890
to Vault, the renewal and
the acquisition of all

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:45.000
of those secrets.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:47.160
And as we'll talk
about in a minute, what

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:50.340
happens when those secrets
are revoked or are rotated?

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:52.670
So I know what you're thinking.

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:54.940
What happens, though, if
my credentials change?

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:56.430
So I have this
sidecar application

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:57.682
that wrote this file out.

00:33:57.682 --> 00:33:59.640
And my application is
reading those credentials

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:00.720
from that file.

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:03.257
But how does this
Consul Template thing

00:34:03.257 --> 00:34:05.340
actually inform my application
that my credentials

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:06.074
have changed?

00:34:06.074 --> 00:34:07.740
So let's think of a
real-world scenario.

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:09.220
I have a database credential.

00:34:09.220 --> 00:34:10.845
Vault tells me that
database credential

00:34:10.845 --> 00:34:12.877
is valid for 24 hours.

00:34:12.877 --> 00:34:14.460
At the end of 24
hours, Vault is going

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:16.800
to revoke that credential,
if it's not revoked earlier

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:18.150
by an operator.

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:21.060
At that point, my application,
or Consul Template,

00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:23.909
has to go back to Vault and
say, OK, I'm still authorized.

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:25.749
I would like another
database credential.

00:34:25.749 --> 00:34:26.790
And it will be different.

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:28.980
It will be a different username
and a different password.

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:30.750
So I'll write that
out to my config file,

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.989
but my application is probably
already loaded its config.

00:34:33.989 --> 00:34:36.750
Most web applications don't go
to disk and read their config

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:37.739
every user request.

00:34:37.739 --> 00:34:40.139
They load that into memory,
and they kind of cache it

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:42.719
until they're restarted or
received some sort of signal.

00:34:42.719 --> 00:34:45.900
So how can Consul Template
signal my application

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:49.350
to reread its credentials,
where, in a Kubernetes world,

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:51.810
where all those
containers are isolated?

00:34:51.810 --> 00:34:56.310
Well, an interesting feature,
and an alpha feature right

00:34:56.310 --> 00:35:00.030
now in Kubernetes is this idea
of shared process namespaces.

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:03.400
So shared process namespaces are
the solution to this challenge.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:04.900
And again, this is
an alpha feature.

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:06.350
So it's available in GKE alpha.

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:08.475
And if you're running your
own Kubernetes clusters,

00:35:08.475 --> 00:35:11.010
you'll have to enable it with
Kubeadm or a similar tool.

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:13.080
But this shared
process namespace

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:15.990
allows our containers to
communicate with one another.

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:19.400
So instead of having every
container be PID 0 or PID 1,

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:21.570
they're actually a
root container and then

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:23.070
these subcontainers
with the ability

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:25.240
to communicate with one another.

00:35:25.240 --> 00:35:28.920
So if we give our
Consul Template process

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:33.480
the SYS_PTRACE sys call, the
security context capability,

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:37.230
we can actually leverage the
standard Unix tools like pidof

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:40.390
and kill to signal applications.

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:43.770
And then, all we have to
do is provide a command

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:44.620
to Consul Template.

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:46.920
And it says, hey, when
this file changes,

00:35:46.920 --> 00:35:48.570
or when this
environment is updated,

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:52.860
or this secret is revoked,
just send, in this example HUP

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:54.410
to the pid of my app.

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:57.480
And we can leverage the
built-in pidof tool for that.

00:35:57.480 --> 00:35:59.820
Or perhaps it's
something more complex.

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.310
We might have some
50-line bash script

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:05.670
that we have to run because
of some arcane Java-based

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:07.557
application that
is very finicky.

00:36:07.557 --> 00:36:09.390
We capture that as code,
and Consul Template

00:36:09.390 --> 00:36:10.770
executes that for us.

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:12.780
So this can either be
as simple as a signal,

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:15.210
or it can be as complex
as a very systematic

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.755
and procedural restart.

00:36:17.755 --> 00:36:20.130
So with that, let's go ahead
and jump over to the laptop,

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:23.470
and we'll take a look
at what demo two is.

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:26.520
So what I'm going to do here is,
in the Google Project Switcher,

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:30.030
I'm actually going to switch
to a different project.

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:32.410
So I have another project
here called My App.

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:34.482
So I'm in a completely
different project now.

00:36:34.482 --> 00:36:35.940
Remember, one of
our best practices

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:38.065
we recommended earlier was
that we have a dedicated

00:36:38.065 --> 00:36:40.500
project and a dedicated
Kubernetes cluster for Vault.

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:43.011
And then, we're only thinking
about Vault as a service

00:36:43.011 --> 00:36:43.510
right now.

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:45.570
It's just a thing
at an IP address.

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:47.250
So I have this other
Kubernetes cluster

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:50.310
where I run all my applications
and services for production.

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:52.145
All my web apps, all
my stateless services,

00:36:52.145 --> 00:36:53.520
all my stateful
services, they're

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:55.955
all in this My Apps
Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:55.955 --> 00:36:57.330
Right now, I don't
have any apps,

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:59.280
but we're going to
create a few in a second.

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:05.930
All right.

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:08.880
So I have a few scripts here.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:13.900
The first is we're going to set
up the Kubernetes auth method.

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:15.560
What the Kubernetes
auth method is

00:37:15.560 --> 00:37:18.320
going to let us do is, like
we described in that diagram,

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:21.290
it's going to configure Vault to
talk to the Kubernetes cluster

00:37:21.290 --> 00:37:23.030
with the right
certificate authority,

00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:26.450
so that we can verify via the
Token Reviewer API, the JWT

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:28.410
tokens.

00:37:28.410 --> 00:37:29.840
So let's go ahead and run that.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:38.670
So it creates a CRB
and a few other things.

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:41.300
And then we actually configure
the Kubernetes auth method,

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:44.990
and we create a new
role for authenticating

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:46.610
to that configuration.

00:37:46.610 --> 00:37:48.680
So now, at a very high
level, what this means

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:51.870
is, when I have a
pod in my cluster,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:54.680
and it goes to Vault
with its service account,

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:56.810
that service account
will be authenticated

00:37:56.810 --> 00:37:58.910
to get a Vault token back
with a predefined policy

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:00.087
that I've configured.

00:38:00.087 --> 00:38:02.420
So this is configuration that
you could capture as code,

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:04.946
or you could run it
manually via the API.

00:38:04.946 --> 00:38:06.320
But then next,
what we want to do

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:09.230
is we want to configure
our GCP secrets.

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:11.510
So the use case here is that--

00:38:11.510 --> 00:38:13.250
Armon's example was
database credentials.

00:38:13.250 --> 00:38:15.980
You might be Postgres
or MySQL, et cetera.

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:18.770
One of the other secrets
engines that we have for GCP

00:38:18.770 --> 00:38:21.890
is the IAM GCP secrets engine.

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:25.490
This lets you programmatically
generate both service accounts

00:38:25.490 --> 00:38:28.050
and service account
keys for GCP.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:29.570
So instead of going
into the web UI,

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:32.450
and clicking, or using
the API directly,

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:35.180
you can programmatically
generate on-the-fly tokens

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:37.970
that are very short lived
for interacting with the GCP

00:38:37.970 --> 00:38:40.820
API, the same way that you might
generate like an AWS access

00:38:40.820 --> 00:38:41.590
key.

00:38:41.590 --> 00:38:43.250
But we can do that
with the GCP API.

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:44.708
So what I've done
here is I've just

00:38:44.708 --> 00:38:49.080
configured Vault, very briefly,
to interact with those systems.

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:50.580
So the last thing
we need to do then

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:53.135
is actually apply
our application.

00:38:53.135 --> 00:38:55.260
So let me show you what
our application looks like.

00:38:58.880 --> 00:39:01.370
So I'm doing a
bunch of stuff here,

00:39:01.370 --> 00:39:04.190
mostly is just because
I'm really bad at typing.

00:39:04.190 --> 00:39:08.396
But I'm grabbing the
credentials from our cluster,

00:39:08.396 --> 00:39:09.770
and then I'm
setting the context,

00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:14.930
and then I'm applying
this Kubernetes YAML file.

00:39:14.930 --> 00:39:17.180
So I'll take a look at
that YAML file here.

00:39:24.107 --> 00:39:26.440
So there are a few things
that I want to point out here.

00:39:26.440 --> 00:39:27.810
The first is that we're
running a pod now.

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.559
We're now no longer
running a StatefulSet.

00:39:29.559 --> 00:39:31.430
This is just like
a straight-up pod.

00:39:31.430 --> 00:39:34.350
It could be replicas, but
there's only one at this point.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:37.057
We're leveraging that shared
process namespace again.

00:39:37.057 --> 00:39:39.390
This is important so that we
can signal our applications

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:42.300
so Consul Template can
talk to our other process.

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:44.100
We have a few volume mounts.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:44.940
We have our secrets.

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:46.140
This is where Consul
Template's actually

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:47.740
going to write its
configurations because there

00:39:47.740 --> 00:39:49.140
are going to be secrets there.

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:50.910
We have our own
TLS configuration.

00:39:50.910 --> 00:39:54.080
This is how our pod talks
to Vault because, again, we

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:57.810
need that certificate authority,
and client-side TLS cert,

00:39:57.810 --> 00:39:59.970
and then the Vault
token, which is

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:04.260
an in-memory volume mount where
the token will be written.

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:06.687
So that never gets
persisted to disk.

00:40:06.687 --> 00:40:09.270
We have our init container here,
which is, again, just pulling

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:11.020
from the Docker Hub.

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:12.630
And we have a few
volume mounts there.

00:40:12.630 --> 00:40:14.420
We set some
environment variables,

00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:16.997
like where is the Vault server,
where is its CA certificate.

00:40:16.997 --> 00:40:18.330
And that's going to run at boot.

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:19.871
And assuming that's
successful, it'll

00:40:19.871 --> 00:40:23.440
put our Vault token at a
well-known path on disk/home

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:26.750
Vault. And at that point,
all of our other containers--

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:28.440
in this example,
Consul Template--

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:34.110
can then pull that token from
the in-memory file system

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:35.177
and interact with it.

00:40:35.177 --> 00:40:36.510
So here we have Consul Template.

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:38.820
We've given it the SYS_PTRACE
capability so that it can

00:40:38.820 --> 00:40:40.634
signal our other application.

00:40:40.634 --> 00:40:42.300
And if we scroll down
here a little bit,

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:45.120
again, same volume mount,
same configuration.

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:47.872
And then here's our Consul
Template configuration.

00:40:47.872 --> 00:40:49.830
And at the very end here,
you'll notice that we

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:52.160
have our arbitrary command.

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:55.080
Our arbitrary command
is just to send kill-HUP

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:57.680
to the pit of our
Vault demo app.

00:40:57.680 --> 00:40:59.610
And then, as you might
suspect, the last thing

00:40:59.610 --> 00:41:04.140
in our configuration here is
our Vault demo app itself,

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:06.900
which is just getting pulled
directly from the Docker Hub.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:09.580
And actually, all it
does is log its own file,

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:11.160
so it's not very exciting.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:13.840
But it's really good for
demonstration purposes.

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:15.740
So let me go ahead
and apply this now.

00:41:19.469 --> 00:41:22.010
So now, if we go back here, we
should see this workload start

00:41:22.010 --> 00:41:22.940
to provision.

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:24.890
And you'll see that the
pod is initializing.

00:41:24.890 --> 00:41:27.280
And if I'm fast enough--

00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:28.580
I wasn't fast enough.

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:30.120
If you're fast
enough, you can see

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:32.430
that it's waiting for the
init container to run.

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:34.405
But if we take a look
at this init container--

00:41:34.405 --> 00:41:36.030
and I'm using
Stackdriver Logging here,

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:38.100
so you'll actually
see the logs directly

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:41.160
in the Google Console UI here.

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:44.970
We see that, a few seconds
ago, that the init container

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:46.740
successfully stored
the Vault token.

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:51.090
So it did that whole walkthrough
of going to the Vault server,

00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:53.070
presenting its JWT
token, interacting

00:41:53.070 --> 00:41:54.180
with the Vault cluster.

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:56.490
And then what we presented
to Consul Template

00:41:56.490 --> 00:41:59.210
was just a Vault token
at a well-known path.

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:01.200
Consul Template then
grabbed that Vault

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:04.710
token and used that token to
interact with the Vault API

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:06.770
to grab our service account key.

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:11.270
So now, when I go back to my
pod and refresh this here,

00:42:11.270 --> 00:42:13.130
we can see that both
Consul Template and My

00:42:13.130 --> 00:42:14.350
App are running.

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:17.030
And if we take a look at
the logs for all of these,

00:42:17.030 --> 00:42:20.330
you'll notice that Consul
Template sent a signal

00:42:20.330 --> 00:42:22.860
to My App causing it to reload.

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:24.945
So at boot, Consul
Template detected a change

00:42:24.945 --> 00:42:26.060
in configuration.

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:28.430
There was no config, and
then it went to some config.

00:42:28.430 --> 00:42:31.310
So it sent the HUP signal
to the Vault demo app.

00:42:31.310 --> 00:42:34.940
The Vault demo app, or My App
in this log, got that signal

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:37.220
and printed out a log
line that it reloaded.

00:42:37.220 --> 00:42:40.370
What you see here is a
soon-to-be-revoked access

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:43.550
credential for interacting with
this particular GCP project.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:47.060
This is an actual IAM access
token for interacting with GCP.

00:42:47.060 --> 00:42:48.020
I'm aware of this.

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:50.567
I will revoke it, but it's cool.

00:42:50.567 --> 00:42:51.900
So I'm showing it to all of you.

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:54.650
And what's happening
is, at some interval,

00:42:54.650 --> 00:42:56.270
this token just
gets printed out,

00:42:56.270 --> 00:42:58.000
basically, every 30 seconds.

00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:01.100
However, at the end of about
five minutes, what will happen

00:43:01.100 --> 00:43:03.620
is Vault will revoke that token.

00:43:03.620 --> 00:43:06.140
So by default-- like
GCP IAM credentials

00:43:06.140 --> 00:43:08.720
live forever until a human
comes in and deletes them.

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:10.910
Vault actually
enforces a lifetime,

00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:14.030
and it makes the necessary
API calls to Google Cloud

00:43:14.030 --> 00:43:16.602
to revoke those credentials
when time permits.

00:43:16.602 --> 00:43:18.560
So, unfortunately, we're
running short on time,

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:21.502
so I can't actually
wait and show you that.

00:43:21.502 --> 00:43:23.710
But if we can switch back
to the slides very quickly.

00:43:26.757 --> 00:43:29.340
So the last section now I'd like
to invite Armon back on stage

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:31.090
to share a little bit
more about the Vault

00:43:31.090 --> 00:43:32.380
roadmap and the future.

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:32.880
So, Armon?

00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:38.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:39.360
ARMON DADGAR: Thank you.

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:42.660
Awesome.

00:43:42.660 --> 00:43:44.460
We are definitely
running short on time.

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:46.830
OK, we're going to
make this light speed.

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:49.932
So thank you, Seth, for walking
us through a little bit more

00:43:49.932 --> 00:43:52.140
hands-on than I had a chance
to go to in terms of how

00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:53.295
we actually work with it.

00:43:53.295 --> 00:43:55.170
Some of the stuff I
wanted to talk about-- so

00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:56.940
for folks that are maybe
familiar with Vault,

00:43:56.940 --> 00:43:57.960
or have already
used it, I wanted

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:59.460
to give a little
bit of a sneak peek

00:43:59.460 --> 00:44:01.980
in terms of where Vault's
going and a little bit

00:44:01.980 --> 00:44:04.350
of our thinking in
terms of directionality.

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:07.560
I think one of the things
that's become clear to us

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:11.190
is that, when we talk about this
space, secret management, tools

00:44:11.190 --> 00:44:13.770
like Vault, integrating it
with these platforms is,

00:44:13.770 --> 00:44:14.820
it's complicated.

00:44:14.820 --> 00:44:16.790
And I think you kind of
saw a snapshot of that

00:44:16.790 --> 00:44:20.809
is, it's not exactly simple and
obvious how all of these things

00:44:20.809 --> 00:44:23.100
kind of come together because
it's a hard problem we're

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:23.850
trying to solve.

00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:26.070
We're trying to solve the
problems of application

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:29.132
identity, brokering between
many different systems that

00:44:29.132 --> 00:44:30.840
don't necessarily
understand each other--

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:34.320
like Postgres has really no
idea what Google Cloud's IAM

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:35.880
model is and vise versa.

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:38.460
So what we're trying to do is
solve this challenge of each

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:41.400
of these systems, implement
different mechanisms of access

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:44.440
control, and we're sort
of brokering between them.

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:45.600
So it's a complex problem.

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:49.090
And so how do we start making
that a little bit simpler?

00:44:49.090 --> 00:44:51.150
So one of the things
we're starting to work on

00:44:51.150 --> 00:44:53.160
within Vault itself is--

00:44:53.160 --> 00:44:55.710
I jokingly refer to
as sort of Clippy

00:44:55.710 --> 00:44:59.580
for Vault. As you can see
a little pop-in wizard

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:01.170
in the corner here,
which says, "It

00:45:01.170 --> 00:45:03.628
looks like you're trying to
configure Vault. Can I help you

00:45:03.628 --> 00:45:04.470
with that?"

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:09.426
But in more seriousness, how
do we help guide users through,

00:45:09.426 --> 00:45:10.800
hey, these are
the different kind

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:12.180
of core workflows
in terms of how

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:14.921
do you configure Vault
to understand Kubernetes

00:45:14.921 --> 00:45:16.170
as an authentication back end?

00:45:16.170 --> 00:45:18.545
How do you configure Vault to
integrate it with Postgres?

00:45:18.545 --> 00:45:20.950
How does the role-mapping
work between this.

00:45:20.950 --> 00:45:24.540
And so it's meant to be
an unobtrusive wizard-like

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:26.160
capability for
different workflows

00:45:26.160 --> 00:45:28.600
that you can enable as
you hit them to say,

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:31.020
hey, please walk me through
how this is supposed to work

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.603
and have it be a little bit more
interactive as you're getting

00:45:33.603 --> 00:45:36.300
started with the system.

00:45:36.300 --> 00:45:38.210
Another common challenge
we see with Vault

00:45:38.210 --> 00:45:39.980
is, how do I go from--

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:42.745
I think Seth did a great
job laying out, what's

00:45:42.745 --> 00:45:44.870
a best practice deployment
guide look like in terms

00:45:44.870 --> 00:45:48.065
of isolating it with a separate
cluster and treating it

00:45:48.065 --> 00:45:52.790
as just opaquely living behind
a load balancer and building HA.

00:45:52.790 --> 00:45:54.170
And so an interesting
pattern is,

00:45:54.170 --> 00:45:57.080
how do we go from providing that
service, Vault as a service,

00:45:57.080 --> 00:45:59.790
for a single application
owner or a single group

00:45:59.790 --> 00:46:01.790
to, all of a sudden, in
many application owners,

00:46:01.790 --> 00:46:02.660
many groups?

00:46:02.660 --> 00:46:06.830
We want Vault as a service
to be highly multitenant

00:46:06.830 --> 00:46:10.250
between many different potential
groups or lines of business.

00:46:10.250 --> 00:46:12.692
And so one approach
to this, and the kind

00:46:12.692 --> 00:46:14.150
of historical
approach, is you lean

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:17.420
on Vault's very fine-grained
capability and an authorization

00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:17.972
system.

00:46:17.972 --> 00:46:20.180
So you can segment out
different parts of the system.

00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:23.001
Maybe you say, [? eng ?] star
is managed by one set of people

00:46:23.001 --> 00:46:25.250
and [? marketing ?] star is
managed by a different set

00:46:25.250 --> 00:46:26.120
of people.

00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:29.150
And we sort of segment using
Vault's hierarchical folder

00:46:29.150 --> 00:46:30.350
structure.

00:46:30.350 --> 00:46:33.200
Another capability, a different
way of thinking about it,

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:34.730
is full namespacing.

00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:37.280
And this is what's coming on
our roadmap is the ability

00:46:37.280 --> 00:46:39.562
to carve out entire
namespaces within Vault

00:46:39.562 --> 00:46:40.520
and say, you know what?

00:46:40.520 --> 00:46:42.230
I don't want to
think about managing

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:43.190
permissions of these folders.

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:44.780
I want to carve out a
whole namespace and say,

00:46:44.780 --> 00:46:47.071
this belongs to engineering,
this belongs to marketing,

00:46:47.071 --> 00:46:48.770
this belongs to another team.

00:46:48.770 --> 00:46:51.560
And those teams can then sub
administer these namespaces

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:52.400
independently.

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:55.250
So looking at, as we go and
think about Vault as a service,

00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:57.440
how do we scale up the
number of consumers,

00:46:57.440 --> 00:47:02.250
the different folks who can be
onbaorded onto this service?

00:47:02.250 --> 00:47:06.360
Lastly is some of the challenge
around integrating with Vault,

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:09.540
as Seth highlighted is there is
this tension between what we'd

00:47:09.540 --> 00:47:11.730
like to be able
to do is maintain

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:14.680
operational flexibility
and not tightly couple

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:15.610
our applications.

00:47:15.610 --> 00:47:18.614
We don't want all of our
applications to embed vault SDK

00:47:18.614 --> 00:47:21.030
and directly talk to the API
and have to be sort of highly

00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:22.410
Vault-aware.

00:47:22.410 --> 00:47:24.660
That sort of limits our
operational flexibility.

00:47:24.660 --> 00:47:26.610
So instead, we'd like
to decouple that and use

00:47:26.610 --> 00:47:29.940
tools like Consul Template
and scripts like Seth's auto

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:32.612
initializater to thread
through the right credentials

00:47:32.612 --> 00:47:34.320
so that our app boots,
reads its secrets,

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:36.170
and it's blissfully unaware.

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:37.680
A really common
challenge, though,

00:47:37.680 --> 00:47:40.230
is how do we do that
authentication before we

00:47:40.230 --> 00:47:41.672
can actually read secrets?

00:47:41.672 --> 00:47:44.130
And so one of the things we're
starting to work on-- and it

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:47.190
actually just shipped
this morning in beta--

00:47:47.190 --> 00:47:48.500
is the Vault Agent.

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:50.400
And so this is an
optional agent you

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:52.920
can choose to run alongside
Vault Server, which

00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:54.027
you have to run.

00:47:54.027 --> 00:47:55.860
And what it does is
manage some of that flow

00:47:55.860 --> 00:47:57.930
for you, the flow
of fetching the JWT,

00:47:57.930 --> 00:48:01.860
authenticating against Vault,
periodically reauthenticating,

00:48:01.860 --> 00:48:03.690
integrating with tools
like Consul Template

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:06.570
to be able to generate and
put configuration on disk

00:48:06.570 --> 00:48:08.970
in a more
application-friendly format.

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:11.610
So these are some of the goals
of the Agent is making it

00:48:11.610 --> 00:48:14.400
easier to authenticate, easier
to manage token renewal,

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:16.590
easier to cache some
of this data locally,

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:19.365
and making those integrations
a little more out-of-the-box.

00:48:19.365 --> 00:48:21.240
So this is something
that just shipped today.

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:23.340
So if this is something
that you find useful,

00:48:23.340 --> 00:48:25.890
please feel free
to play with it.

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:28.642
So with that, thank you, guys
so much for sitting through

00:48:28.642 --> 00:48:29.600
with us and joining us.

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:32.630
And thank you to Seth as
well for helping with this.

00:48:32.630 --> 00:48:38.335
[MUSIC PLAYING]

